              "t": "sets",
              "sets": 4,
              "reps": 5,
              "note": "4×5 · 2 min rest",
              "rest": 120
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Carries",
          "timer": {
            "label": "2 min clock ×3",
            "ph": [
              [
                "Round",
                120
              ]
            ],
            "rep": 3
          },
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "farmer",
              "n": "Farmer's carry",
              "t": "rounds",
              "rounds": 3,
              "note": "40 yd · heavy",
              "wt": true,
              "how": "Hold a heavy dumbbell or handle in each hand. Stand tall, shoulders back, brace your stomach. Walk 40 yd with short, quick steps without setting them down. Pick a weight that's hard to hold by the end. Log the weight in one hand.",
              "wprog": {
                "inc": 10
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "suitcase",
              "n": "Suitcase carry",
              "t": "rounds",
              "rounds": 3,
              "note": "20 yd each side",
              "wt": true,
              "how": "One weight in one hand only. Walk 20 yd without leaning toward the weight: stay level, as if it weren't there. Switch hands and walk 20 yd back. That's one round. It trains your core to resist bending sideways.",
              "wprog": {
                "inc": 5
              }
            }
          ],
          "about": "Why the clock: it sets your pace. Each time a new 2-minute round starts, do one farmer's carry and one suitcase carry (both hands), then rest for whatever's left of the 2 minutes. 3 rounds. It keeps rest short enough to stay honest but long enough to carry heavy."
        },
        {
          "label": "Sled",
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "sled",
              "n": "Sled push / drag",
              "t": "rounds",
              "rounds": 4,
              "note": "Push 20 yd, drag 20 yd back",
              "wt": true,
              "how": "One round = push the sled 20 yd, then drag it 20 yd back to the start. Push: hands on the handles, arms straight, body leaning in, drive with short powerful steps. Drag: hold the straps or handles, face the sled and walk backward, staying low in your legs. No sled? Do 10 walking lunges each leg per round. Log the weight you put on the sled.",
              "wprog": {
                "inc": 20
              }
            }
          ],
          "about": "No clock here. Rest about 60–90 sec between rounds, enough to push hard every time."
        },
        {
          "label": "Finisher",
          "timer": {
            "label": "30 on / 30 off",
            "ph": [
              [
                "On",
                30
              ],
              [
                "Off",
                30
              ]
            ],
            "rep": 8
          },
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "finisher",
              "n": "30-on / 30-off finisher",
              "t": "rounds",
              "rounds": 8,
              "note": "Bike, row or sled · hard",
              "how": "Hard, not all-out: go at about 8–9 out of 10, a pace you can repeat for all 8 rounds. On the 30 off, keep moving slowly; don't stop. If rounds 6–8 fall apart, start a bit easier next time. If round 8 feels easy, push harder. Use a bike, rower or sled and stick with the same one each week so you can compare."
            }
          ],
          "about": "Why the clock: 30 sec hard, 30 sec easy, 8 times (8 minutes). Start it and it beeps at every switch, so you never have to watch the time."
        }
      ]
